  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A small block slides down from the top of hemisphere of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> as shown in the figure. The height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math>&nbsp;at which the block will lose contact with the surface of the sphere is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>. (Assume there is no friction between the block and the hemisphere)</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0072_e29e24805591.png\" style=\"width: 367px; height: 183px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\st0072_dc3f28099b2b.png\" style=\"width: 407px; height: 173px;\" /></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">R</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>Energy conservation</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>{</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>}</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p><p>&nbsp;from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mfenced close=\"}\" open=\"{\"><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mi>R</mi></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>{</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math></p>"
  }
